THE ONE MISS -- AN APPARATUS PROPERTY, MEASURED, NOT A MUTANT BEHAVIOUR.  Under M_BIT30 the all-ones session ends the
emulator at the read (an exit with NO diagnostic).  The probe then still types `quit' into the pty, and the line was
echoed WITHOUT the `GXemul> ' prompt, because nothing was left to print one; the grammar check counted that bare `quit'
as off-grammar output (probe_M_BIT30.raw: `noise: ['quit']').  M_EQ4 dies the same way and did NOT show it -- whether the
write lands before the pty closes is TIMING.  So the extra red can appear only on a leg whose process has ALREADY DIED,
i.e. a leg some value row has already killed; it cannot appear on a live process, where `quit' always arrives as
`GXemul> quit' (every BASE and every surviving session in both tables).  Recorded rather than patched: changing the
grammar mid-table would have given the table two instruments.  The noise lines of every other red `read silent' are the
emulator's own diagnostics (probe_*.raw `noise:' lines): the TODO text, or the default arm's UNIMPLEMENTED read under
the ALIAS legs.

FILED, not fixed: the quiet grammar could also admit a bare echo of the probe's OWN commands (`quit', `print rN', ...)
from a dead pty, which would make the silence row independent of that timing.  Not owed: it removes only a red that a
value row already carries.
